\chapter{S-Expressions: Reader, Printer, and Rule Notation}
\label{ch:sexpressions}

A rewrite system needs a surface syntax. Rules must come from somewhere: a
configuration file, a test fixture, a literate program, a command-line
argument, an editor buffer. If the syntax of a rule is baked into the
compiler, the compiler owns a grammar that every user of the library must
agree on. If the syntax is a plain string of parentheses, the library owns
almost nothing and the user owns everything, including the decision of
whether rules are written in Lisp, in Scheme, in a domain-specific notation
that happens to be parenthesised, or in some entirely unrelated surface
syntax of their own devising.

DSLtk resolves this tension with the S-expression. It is the smallest
interchange language that can carry a tree of atoms, and it is exactly
large enough to carry a rewrite rule, a grammar production, and a term. The
library provides a reader for it, a printer for it, a bridge to the
\code{ASTNode} tree of \chapref{ch:astnode}, and a small notation for
rules that is shared with the equality-saturation machinery of
\chapref{ch:egraphs}. This chapter describes that layer in full, because
everything downstream---the e-graph, the rule notation, the equality
observers---is expressed in it.

The single most important thing to understand about the DSLtk S-expression is
that it is \emph{not} a tagged union, and that quoting is a first-class
property of the value rather than something the printer infers. Those two
facts drive almost every design decision in the rest of this chapter.

\section{The data model}

\api{dsl::SExpr} is a plain aggregate with four public data members
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9142). The following listing is an abridged
excerpt: it shows the data members only, and the two factories and the
printer follow them in the header.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The four data members of \code{SExpr}.},label=lst:sexpr-struct]
struct SExpr
{
  bool is_atom = true;
  std::string text;
  std::vector<SExpr> items;
  bool quoted = false;
  // ...
};
\end{lstlisting}

The type is a \emph{tagged struct, not a variant}. There is no
\code{std::variant}, no \code{std::optional} of a list, and no
\code{std::visit}. A value is an atom if \code{is_atom} is true and a list
otherwise; the list's elements live in \code{items} and the atom's spelling
lives in \code{text}. This is worth stating plainly because the natural
instinct, coming from a modern C++ background, is to reach for a variant and
then to be surprised that the code does not compile. It is also worth
stating that \code{is_atom} is a \emph{data member, not a method}. It
reads as \code{expr.is_atom}, not \code{expr.is_atom()}. A
\code{!is_atom} member means the value is a list.

Because the type is an aggregate, the two factories are \emph{static member
functions} rather than constructors, and the aggregate initialisation they
perform is visible in the source:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The two factories, showing the auto-escalation rule.},label=lst:sexpr-factories]
static SExpr
atom (std::string text, bool quoted = false)
{
  if (text.empty ())
    quoted = true;
  for (unsigned char c : text)
    quoted |= c <= ' ' || c == '(' || c == ')' || c == '"' || c == ';'
              || c == '\\';
  return { true, std::move (text), {}, quoted };
}
static SExpr
list (std::vector<SExpr> items)
{
  return { false, {}, std::move (items), false };
}
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{atom} factory performs \emph{auto-escalation}: the caller may ask
for an unquoted atom, but the factory overrides the request and forces
\code{quoted} to true whenever the text is empty or contains any byte that
is less than or equal to a space, or one of \code{(}, \code{)}, \code{"},
\code{;}, or \texttt{\textbackslash}. The reason is mechanical: an unquoted atom
is printed bare, and an atom printed bare cannot contain a delimiter without
changing the meaning of the output. Rather than silently producing a
string that will not read back as the same value, the factory guarantees
the round-trip property \code{parse_sexpr (e.dump ()).dump () == e.dump ()}
for every value it can build. The \code{list} factory always produces an
unquoted list, because a list's quoting is a property of its elements.

The \code{quoted} flag carries a second, semantic meaning that the printer
and the rule machinery both rely on: \emph{a quoted atom is data, never a
pattern variable}. The rule notation of \secref{sec:rule-notation} uses
atoms whose text begins with a question mark as pattern variables. A bare
\code{?x} therefore denotes a metavariable, while \code{"?x"} denotes the
two-character string \code{?x}. The distinction is recorded in the data
member and survives every round trip through the reader, the printer, and
the equality graph.

The equality operator is \code{operator== (const SExpr &) const = default},
so it compares all four members. Values built through the normal factories
preserve their meaningful atom/list and quoting distinctions. Values that
differ only in quoting are not equal: \code{SExpr::atom
("x")} and \code{SExpr::atom ("x", true)} are distinct S-expressions, even
though their printed forms differ as well. There is no explicit \code{operator!=};
the defaulted \code{==} supplies it in \cpp{}20, but it is the four-field
comparison that matters, not the absence of a spelling.

