\section{Printing}

The printer is a single member function, \code{dump}, and it is worth
reading closely because its output is the canonical text form that the
reader is expected to invert. A list is printed as a parenthesised,
space-separated sequence: \code{(a b c)}. An atom is printed bare when it is
safe and double-quoted when it is not. The decision is made twice, once at
construction time by the auto-escalation rule and once at print time, and
the two agree because the print-time test is the same predicate
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, lines 9178--9181): quote the atom if it was flagged, if
it is empty, or if it contains a byte that is at most a space, or one of
\code{(}, \code{)}, \code{"}, \code{;}, or \texttt{\textbackslash}.

Inside the quotes, five characters are escaped: newline becomes
\texttt{\textbackslash n}, carriage return becomes \texttt{\textbackslash r}, tab
becomes \texttt{\textbackslash t}, a double quote becomes
\texttt{\textbackslash"}, and a backslash becomes \texttt{\textbackslash\textbackslash}.
Every other byte, including a byte with the high bit set, is emitted
verbatim. Note that the escape set is exactly the set the reader accepts,
which is what makes the printer and the reader inverses of one another.

Two consequences deserve emphasis. First, the printer always quotes an
atom that was constructed as quoted, even when the text would be perfectly
safe unquoted: \code{SExpr::atom ("?x", true).dump ()} yields
\code{"?x"}, not \code{?x}. This is deliberate. The quoted flag is
semantic, and a printer that silently dropped it would turn a data atom
into a pattern variable on the next read. Second, an atom that was not
flagged but whose text would be unsafe is quoted at print time anyway,
which is a belt-and-braces measure for values that were built by aggregate
initialisation rather than by the factory.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslsexpr,caption=Canonical printed forms.,label=lst:sexpr-dumps]
;; bare atoms stay bare
(add a 0)          ; the list (add a 0)
mul                ; the atom mul
;; atoms that need quoting get it
"a b"              ; the atom a<space>b
""                 ; the empty atom
"a;b"              ; the atom a;s
"a\\b"             ; the atom a\b
;; the quoted flag survives
"?x"               ; data, not a variable
?x                 ; a variable
\end{lstlisting}

\section{The reader}

Reading is the job of \api{dsl::sexpr_detail::Reader}, a small struct
holding a \code{std::string_view source} and a \code{std::size_t pos}
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9213). The struct is in a \code{sexpr_detail}
namespace, which is the library's convention for machinery that is
deliberately not part of the public interface; the public entry points are
free functions that construct a reader and drive it.

The reader has three member functions. \code{skip()} consumes whitespace
and comments. \code{error()} throws. \code{read (depth)} consumes exactly
one S-expression and returns it.

\code{skip()} treats any byte at most a space as whitespace, and treats a
semicolon as the start of a line comment that runs to the end of the line.
There are \emph{no block comments}: there is no \texttt{\#} form, no nested
comment syntax, and no way to comment out a region. The reason is that the
S-expression notation is meant to be embedded inside other host languages,
where \texttt{\#} is frequently already spoken for, and where a line comment
is the one form that cannot be confused with the host's own syntax.

\code{error()} throws \code{std::invalid_argument} with a message of the
form \code{S-expression at N: ...}, where \code{N} is the reader's current
offset into the source. The offset is the position of the offending byte,
which for a nested failure is the position at which the reader was when it
detected the problem, not necessarily the position of the outermost
unbalanced parenthesis. This is a deliberate trade: the message identifies
the point of failure precisely, and the reader does not maintain a stack of
open parentheses to report the enclosing context.

\code{read (depth)} is the heart of the reader. It is recursive, and the
\code{depth} parameter is a guard rather than a hint: if \code{depth}
exceeds 512 the reader raises \code{nesting limit exceeded}. The counter
starts at zero for a top-level form, so a source containing 513 levels of
nested parentheses is rejected, while 512 levels are accepted. The limit
exists because the reader recurses on the C++ stack, and an unbounded
nesting limit would turn a malformed or hostile input into a stack
overflow, which is a crash rather than a diagnosable error.

The reader dispatches on the next significant byte. A \code{(} opens a
list: the reader consumes it and then loops, reading sub-expressions until
it encounters a \code{)}, which it consumes and returns as a list. Running
out of input inside a list raises \code{unterminated list}. A \code{"}
opens a quoted atom, which is read until the closing quote with the escape
rules described below. Anything else begins a bare atom, which runs until
whitespace, a \code{(}, a \code{)}, or a \code{;}. A \code{)} encountered
where an expression was expected raises \code{unexpected ')'}.

The quoted-atom reader recognises exactly five escapes: \texttt{\textbackslash n},
\texttt{\textbackslash r}, \texttt{\textbackslash t}, \texttt{\textbackslash\textbackslash},
and \texttt{\textbackslash"}. A backslash at the very end of the source raises
\code{unfinished escape}; a backslash followed by anything else raises
\code{unknown escape}. A quoted atom that is never closed raises
\code{unterminated quoted atom}.

One point deserves a warning, because the notation it contradicts is
ubiquitous in Lisp-family languages: \textbf{the \code{'} quote form is not
supported}. Writing \code{'x} does not produce the atom \code{x} quoted;
it produces the bare atom \code{'x}, because a single quote is not a
delimiter and the bare-atom scanner simply consumes it as an ordinary
byte. This is not an oversight in the reader but a consequence of the
grammar: the reader has exactly two ways to write an atom, a bare one and
a double-quoted one.

