\begin{pseudocode}[Read one S-expression]
\textbf{function} read (reader, depth)\\
\quad if depth is greater than 512 then\\
\qquad raise \emph{nesting limit exceeded}\\
\quad skip whitespace and \code{;} comments\\
\quad if the source is exhausted then\\
\qquad raise \emph{expected expression}\\
\quad if the next byte is \code{)} then\\
\qquad raise \emph{unexpected ')'}\\
\quad if the next byte is \code{(} then\\
\qquad consume it\\
\qquad repeat\\
\qquad\qquad skip whitespace and comments\\
\qquad\qquad if the source is exhausted then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad raise \emph{unterminated list}\\
\qquad\qquad if the next byte is \code{)} then\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad consume it and return the list\\
\qquad\qquad append read (reader, depth plus one) to the items\\
\quad if the next byte is \code{"} then\\
\qquad consume it and read characters until the closing quote\\
\qquad\qquad apply the five escapes; reject any other escape\\
\qquad return a quoted atom\\
\quad otherwise\\
\qquad scan bytes until space, \code{(}, \code{)}, or \code{;}\\
\qquad return a bare atom
\end{pseudocode}

\section{The public parsing functions}

Two free functions expose the reader. \api{parse_sexpressions} takes a
\code{std::string_view} and returns a \code{std::vector<SExpr>}: it reads
every form in the text, in order, and returns an empty vector for an empty
or comment-only input. \api{parse_sexpr} takes the same argument and
returns a single \code{SExpr}; if the text does not contain exactly one
form it throws \code{std::invalid_argument} whose message is
\emph{expected exactly one S-expression}. The distinction matters: \code{parse_sexpressions} is the
entry point for a file of rules or a file of top-level forms, while
\code{parse_sexpr} is the entry point for a value that is expected to be a
single tree, such as the left-hand side of a rewrite.

Neither function owns its argument. Both take a \code{std::string_view},
so a caller may pass a string literal, a \code{std::string}, or a slice of
a larger buffer, and the S-expressions that come back own their own
storage: the reader copies the text of each atom into a fresh
\code{std::string}, so the result outlives the source buffer. That is one
reason the reader's input is a view but the output is not.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=Reading S-expressions and printing them back.,label=lst:sexpr-roundtrip]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int
main ()
{
  const std::string source = R"(
; A tiny program in the interchange language.
(define (square x) (* x x))
(print (square 7))
(print "a quoted atom")
)";

  std::cout << "== read and print back ==\n";
  for (const auto &form : dsl::parse_sexpressions (source))
    std::cout << "  " << form.dump () << "\n";

  std::cout << "\n== quoting is a data member ==\n";
  auto pattern = dsl::parse_sexpr ("(mul (add ?x 0) 1)");
  const auto &variable = pattern.items[1].items[1];
  std::cout << "  ?x text        : " << variable.text << "\n";
  std::cout << "  ?x quoted?     : " << std::boolalpha << variable.quoted << "\n";
  auto literal = dsl::SExpr::atom ("?x", true);
  std::cout << "  \"?x\" quoted?   : " << literal.quoted << "\n";
  std::cout << "  equal?         : " << (variable == literal) << "\n";
  std::cout << "  auto-escalated : " << dsl::SExpr::atom ("a b").dump () << "\n";

  std::cout << "\n== exactly one form ==\n";
  try
    {
      (void) dsl::parse_sexpr ("(a) (b)");
    }
  catch (const std::exception &ex)
    {
      std::cout << "  " << ex.what () << "\n";
    }
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output of \lstref{lst:sexpr-roundtrip} is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption=Output of \lstref{lst:sexpr-roundtrip}.]
== read and print back ==
  (define (square x) (* x x))
  (print (square 7))
  (print "a quoted atom")

== quoting is a data member ==
  ?x text        : ?x
  ?x quoted?     : false
  "?x" quoted?   : true
  equal?         : false
  auto-escalated : "a b"

== exactly one form ==
  expected exactly one S-expression
\end{lstlisting}

\section{The bridge to \code{ASTNode}}

Two functions translate between the interchange form and the value-type tree
of \chapref{ch:astnode}. \api{ast_to_sexpr} maps a leaf to a two-element
list \code{(tag value)} and an inner node to \code{(tag child ...)},
with the tag as the head atom (\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9332). The
conversion is therefore total: every \code{ASTNode} has an S-expression
form, and the form is unambiguous, because a leaf is always a two-element
list whose second element is an atom.

\api{sexpr_to_ast} is the inverse, and it is where the asymmetry of the
representation shows. The implementation is at \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line
9343. The head of the list must be an atom. A list whose head is itself a
list is rejected: the adapter throws \code{std::invalid_argument}, and the
message is \emph{AST S-expression requires an atom head}.

A two-element list whose second element is an atom is interpreted as a
leaf, and every other list is interpreted as an inner node whose children
are the recursively converted tail.

The ambiguity is real and unavoidable: a two-element list
\code{(num 1)} could mean the leaf \code{num} with value \code{1} or the
inner node \code{num} with one child, the atom \code{1}. The bridge
resolves it in favour of the leaf reading, which matches the convention
that leaves are tagged with a value and inner nodes are tagged with
children. A user who needs the other reading must keep the S-expression
rather than round-tripping through \code{ASTNode}. The older prose in the
DSLtk manual says the same thing and it is worth repeating: the
interchange model and the tree model are not isomorphic, and the bridge is a
convenience, not a bijection.

