\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{The rule notation}
\label{sec:rule-notation}

The same interchange language carries rewrite rules, and the notation is
deliberately small. A rule is one of three shapes:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslsexpr,caption=The three accepted rule shapes.,label=lst:rule-shapes]
(rewrite name lhs rhs)   ; a named rule
(= lhs rhs)              ; an anonymous equality, named "="
(=> lhs rhs)             ; an anonymous equality, named "=>"
\end{lstlisting}

\api{sexpr_rule} accepts either a parsed \code{SExpr} or a
\code{std::string_view} that it parses itself, and returns an
\api{ERewriteRule}, which is the triple \code{\{ std::string name; SExpr lhs,
rhs; \}}. The two anonymous forms take their name from the operator text, so
\code{(= lhs rhs)} yields a rule named \code{=} and \code{(=> lhs rhs)} one
named \code{=>}. The named form requires the second element to be an atom,
which supplies the rule's name; a name that happens to look like a
variable is nevertheless accepted as a name, since names are never
interpreted.

Any other shape raises \code{std::invalid_argument} whose message is
\emph{rule syntax is (rewrite name lhs rhs) or (= lhs rhs)}. In particular, a four-element
list whose head is \code{rewrite} but whose second element is not an atom
is rejected, and so is a three-element list whose head is neither \code{=}
nor \code{=>}.

\api{sexpr_rules} parses a whole file: it reads every form in the text and
converts each one into a rule, so a rule file is a sequence of rule forms
with no enclosing parentheses of their own.

\subsection{Variables and validation}

A pattern variable is an unquoted atom whose text begins with \code{?} and
is at least two characters long. The helper \api{is_pattern_variable}
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9370) encodes exactly that:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=What counts as a pattern variable.,label=lst:is-pattern-variable]
inline bool
is_pattern_variable (const SExpr &term)
{
  return term.is_atom && !term.quoted && term.text.size () > 1
         && term.text[0] == '?';
}
\end{lstlisting}

Three conditions matter. The atom must not be quoted, which is how
\code{"?x"} is kept as data. The text must be longer than one character, so
a bare \code{?} is an ordinary atom and not a variable. And the text must
begin with \code{?}. Nothing else is special: \code{?x-1} is a variable
name, and two occurrences of \code{?x} in one pattern denote the same
variable.

The validator \api{validate_eterm} walks a term and collects its
variables. It rejects two things. A list whose head is not an atom is
invalid, and a list whose head \emph{is} a pattern variable is also
invalid: both raise \code{std::invalid_argument} whose message is
\emph{e-graph term requires a literal operator head}. The head of a term is its operator name, and
operators are literal: an e-graph node is keyed by an operator string, so
there is no way to represent a node whose operator is chosen by a match.
Variables may nevertheless occupy any complete term position, including
the entire left-hand side, so \code{(= ?x ?x)} is a legal, if degenerate,
rule.

After both sides are validated, the rule constructor checks that every
variable occurring on the right-hand side also occurs on the left-hand
side. If not, it raises \code{std::invalid_argument} whose message is
\emph{unbound RHS variable X}, where \code{X} is the offending variable's text. The check
is not merely syntactic tidiness. A right-hand variable that the
left-hand side never binds has no meaning: the matcher would have to
invent a binding for it, and different implementations invent different
ones. Rejecting the rule at construction time turns a silent misbehaviour
into an immediate, precisely located error.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Parsing and validating rules. The fragment assumes the includes of \lstref{lst:sexpr-roundtrip}.},label=lst:sexpr-rules]
auto rules = dsl::sexpr_rules ("(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)\n"
                                "(= (mul ?x 1) ?x)\n");
for (const auto &rule : rules)
  std::cout << rule.name << " : " << rule.lhs.dump () << " => "
            << rule.rhs.dump () << "\n";

try
  {
    (void) dsl::sexpr_rule ("(= (add ?x 0) ?y)");
  }
catch (const std::exception &ex)
  {
    std::cout << ex.what () << "\n";   // unbound RHS variable ?y
  }
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{notebox}[The rule set is not an operator table]
The rule notation shares its \emph{grammar} with the interchange language
but not its \emph{alphabet}. The heads \code{rewrite}, \code{=}, and
\code{=>} are reserved in rule position only; they remain ordinary atoms
everywhere else, including in the left-hand and right-hand sides of a rule.
A term whose operator is literally named \code{=} is perfectly legal, which
is exactly the sort of corner that a separate, richer rule language would
have had to forbid.
\end{notebox}

\section{Edge cases and limits}

The following behaviours are worth stating explicitly, because each of them
is a place where a reader might reasonably expect a different answer.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{No \code{'} quote.} \code{'x} reads as the bare atom
  \code{'x}. There is no mechanism in the grammar for it, and adding one
  would have required choosing between the Lisp \code{'} and the Scheme
  \code{'} semantics.
\item \textbf{No block comments.} Only \code{;} line comments exist. A
  \texttt{\#} is an ordinary atom character.
\item \textbf{Depth 512.} A form nested 513 levels deep is rejected with
  \emph{nesting limit exceeded}. The limit protects the C++ stack, since
  the reader recurses once per level.
\item \textbf{Five escapes only.} An unknown escape is an error, not a
  literal. There is no hexadecimal or octal escape syntax.
\item \textbf{Exactly one form.} \code{parse_sexpr} refuses a text with
  zero forms or with two or more, and the error message does not say which
  case occurred.
\item \textbf{Comments inside a form.} Because \code{skip()} is called
  between every element of a list, a comment may appear anywhere a
  whitespace may appear, including between the head of a list and its
  first element.
\item \textbf{Empty lists.} \code{()} is a valid S-expression and a valid
  list, but it is \emph{not} a valid e-graph term, because a term must
  have a literal operator head.
\item \textbf{Quoting is not canonicalised.} A quoted atom prints quoted
  even when quoting is unnecessary, and an unquoted atom whose text is
  unsafe prints quoted. Both behaviours are stable under re-reading, so a
  printed form always reads back to an equal value.
\item \textbf{Atoms are copied.} The reader takes a view but produces owned
  strings, so results outlive their source; conversely, reading a very
  large text allocates a \code{std::string} per atom.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The S-expression layer is small enough to read in one sitting and
opinionated enough to be worth reading. Its two load-bearing decisions are
that a value is a tagged struct rather than a variant, and that quoting is
data. Everything else in this chapter follows from those. The next chapter
takes the rule notation of \secref{sec:rule-notation} and gives it an
execution model: an equality graph in which every rule application adds
information rather than replacing a tree.
